{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ConcurrencyAndCancellation","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/concurrencyandcancellation"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"code":"@MainActor","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and built under Swift 6 strict concurrency. Every ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadWithRetry","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"loadNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" call is ","type":"text"},{"code":"async","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", which raises three questions the moment a screen does more than one of them: what happens if a second call starts before the first finishes? How do you stop one early? And how do you force a store back to a known state outside of any of them? This article covers all three.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Overlapping calls only ever apply the latest result","anchor":"Overlapping-calls-only-ever-apply-the-latest-result","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Nothing extra is needed to handle a screen firing off a second call before an earlier one has finished — a fast double-tap on Retry, or a new search query arriving before the previous one resolved:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 1) } \/\/ slow — still in flight","await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: 2) } \/\/ fast — resolves first"],"syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Internally, every call stamps itself with a token before running ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and only applies its result — ","type":"text"},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", or the ","type":"text"},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" mapping ","type":"text"},{"code":"loadCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" do — if that token is still the most recently started one by the time ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" resolves. In the example above, article 1’s slower fetch resolving ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"after"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" article 2’s result is already showing never overwrites it; its result is silently dropped. ","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" follows the same rule: it only clears once the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"newest"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" in-flight refresh finishes, not whichever overlapping one happens to resolve first. ","type":"text"},{"code":"loadNextPage(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"isLoadingNextPage","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" follows it too.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"You don’t need to call ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","type":"reference"},{"text":" for this to hold — it’s automatic. Reach for ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when you additionally want to stop the older call’s work from running at all, not just prevent its result from landing.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Cancelling explicitly","anchor":"Cancelling-explicitly","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Call ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/cancel()","type":"reference"},{"text":" to stop whatever call is currently running, without waiting for it to resolve:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["store.cancel() \/\/ e.g. from onDisappear, or right before firing off a newer query"],"syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (and ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") are left exactly as they were — cancelling never surfaces a ","type":"text"},{"code":"CancellationError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" through ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" the way letting ","type":"text"},{"code":"operation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" itself fail would. This applies uniformly, including to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/loadWithRetry(maxAttempts:backoff:_:)","type":"reference"},{"text":" cancelled mid-backoff: it stops retrying and leaves ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" untouched, just like every other call. If nothing is in flight, ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" does nothing.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Cancelling automatically","anchor":"Cancelling-automatically","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A store also cancels whatever it was still tracking when it’s deallocated, as a last-resort safety net — you don’t need to call ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" from ","type":"text"},{"code":"deinit","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" yourself. In practice this rarely has anything to do: ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/etc. already ","type":"text"},{"code":"await","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" their own work to completion before returning, so by the time a store can actually be deallocated, there’s normally nothing left in flight to cancel. It exists to guard against that assumption changing later, for free.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Resetting outside of a load","anchor":"Resetting-outside-of-a-load","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore\/reset(to:)","type":"reference"},{"text":" snaps a store back to ","type":"text"},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (or any state you give it) without going through ","type":"text"},{"code":"load","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refresh","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" at all — useful for a full sign-out flow, where whatever the store was showing or fetching no longer applies to the next signed-in user:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["func signOut() {","    session.clear()","    articlesStore.reset() \/\/ back to .loading, as if freshly created","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Like ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", it stops whatever operation is currently in flight; unlike ","type":"text"},{"code":"cancel()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", it also immediately replaces ","type":"text"},{"code":"state","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and clears ","type":"text"},{"code":"isRefreshing","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so a screen bound to the store reflects the reset right away instead of waiting for a cancelled operation to unwind.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"metadata":{"role":"article","title":"Concurrency & Cancellation","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article"},"abstract":[{"text":"The guarantees ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference"},{"text":" makes about overlapping, cancelled, and reset calls.","type":"text"}],"sections":[],"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"],"title":"Related Documentation","anchor":"Related-Documentation"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStatePreviewError","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Pagination"],"title":"Modeling state","generated":true,"anchor":"Modeling-state"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"kind":"article","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI":{"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/gettingstartedwithswiftui","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","title":"Getting Started with SwiftUI","role":"article","kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"Drive a SwiftUI screen’s four states with ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation